#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153118} :activity/id #uuid "29345f23-5e83-5b04-afba-830c11338a90" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051041 :topic/id #uuid "7c0a3263-cc36-57b6-839b-6ff5594cae61" :topic/math-academy-id 2678} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153387 :tutorial/content "A function $f: X\\rightarrow Y$ is an **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f: X \\to Y$ is an injection if $\\forall\\, x_1, x_2 \\in X,$ if $f(x_1)=f(x_2),$ then $x_1=x_2.$\n\nWe can read the above statement as follows:\n\n*The function $f$ that maps from the domain $X$ to the codomain $Y$ is an injection if, for all $x_1$ and $x_2$ contained within the domain $X,$ if $f(x_1)$ and $f(x_2)$ are equal, then $x_1$ and $x_2$ are equal*\n\nIn other words, a function is an injection if no two different elements of the domain map to the same element of the range.\n\nFor example, the following diagram shows an example of an injection.\n\n![](images/43/437dc5ed10f647d2a216ca1ce16aeedd007063f473e0fb9aa8e72b58f22fa292.png)\n\nHowever, the following is **not** an injection because there are two different elements in the domain ($1$ and $2$) that map to the same element of the range ($-1$).\n\n![](images/2d/2d9e2540f949ad6ee3daa6c8236342a9e4b7c6349402c55c79c71be67162df48.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6f4bd79b-5ff6-5d9c-ab61-78957d53057b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4194 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "49c27874-4216-5f46-87e3-47eed303d536" :step/math-academy-id 25449 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153119}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152004 :question/id #uuid "4fa1e488-75e9-5da8-9a07-c1fa4c797531" :question/math-academy-id "e-6869"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c805415f-c87d-50c5-b668-948fc46bcc7e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050318}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385698}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"} :step/id #uuid "9403daa8-8f39-5ff4-a903-7b35be698e09" :step/math-academy-id 25450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153135}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152201 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152006 :question/id #uuid "9898b130-1ef8-5c59-a6de-dae8640155ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-6871"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f45ad8ed-5b6d-5e46-b337-02ab271013b3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049626} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050724}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407919}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections From Functions Given Algebraically"} :step/id #uuid "789f00d2-d056-5151-913d-3ecb5a85b5ed" :step/math-academy-id 25452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153127 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153396 :tutorial/content "How do we determine whether a function $f$ is an injection without using a graph or mapping diagram?\n\nTo do this, we take $x_1$ and $x_2$ (arbitrary but fixed) in the domain of $f,$ and we construct the equation $f(x_1)=f(x_2).$ Using algebraic manipulation, we aim to get some relation between the variables $x_1$ and $x_2.$ If we get $x_1=x_2,$ this implies that $f$ is an injection.\n\nLet's use this idea to prove that $f:\\mathbb{R}\\rightarrow (0,\\infty)$ defined by $f(x)=2^x$ is an injection.\n\nFirst, we construct the equation $f(x_1) = f(x_2).$ This gives $$\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, using algebraic manipulation, we reduce this relationship to $x_1=x_2,$ as follows: $$\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{2}({2}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={log}_{2}({2}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, for any $x_1, x_2 \\in\\mathbb{R},$ if $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$ we have $x_1=x_2.$ In other words, $f$ is an injection." :tutorial/id #uuid "6592b1a8-cfe6-5f38-be26-79e350169bf6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4229 :tutorial/title "Identifying Injections Defined on Arbitrary Sets"} :step/id #uuid "186077d7-1922-5507-80c3-e962b24bfb61" :step/math-academy-id 25650 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153134}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153128 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152202 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152009 :question/id #uuid "0d641eb0-a77d-5c36-a232-8a03486ddd49" :question/math-academy-id "e-6949"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a347133c-5e5a-5dd2-89ef-bdd14245ee6b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050300}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407921}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "557e7c85-65bb-549c-ae5d-8e48fdaa9be7" :step/math-academy-id 25651 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153129}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152203 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152010 :question/id #uuid "98e5e792-4ff6-5555-af05-b3a7e4655053" :question/math-academy-id "e-6950"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99c2db8c-670b-521b-8942-3b89f1e95d3f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407920}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Injections For Functions Whose Domain or Codomain Are Real Intervals"} :step/id #uuid "216a5b7d-dc96-5cff-97b4-eea712552a91" :step/math-academy-id 25652 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153127}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153134 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153399 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that $Y$ is a relatively small finite set, and we want to know if a function $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an injection. All we need to do is go through each element $x$ in $X$ ensure that they each map to *distinct* elements of $Y.$\n\nFor example, consider the function $f: \\{-1,0,1\\} \\to \\{{\\color{blue}0},{\\color{red}4}\\}$ defined by $f(x) =4x^2.$ Is it an injection?\n\nSince the domain $X$ is a small, finite set, it's easy to compute the range.\n\n$$f(-1)={\\color{red}4},\\quad f(0)={\\color{blue}0}, \\quad f(1)={\\color{red}4}.$$\n\nNote that $f(-1) = f(1) = {\\color{red}4},$ but $-1\\neq 1.$ So, the elements in the domain $X$ do not map to distinct elements in $Y.$\n\nTherefore, $f$ is not an injection." :tutorial/id #uuid "339a2c21-49d8-5fc7-b2ba-5d9dbcb242dc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4309 :tutorial/title "Identifying Injections When the Domain is a Small Finite Set"} :step/id #uuid "f9438c8c-4c2e-5c84-bef4-cdbef3e05650" :step/math-academy-id 26197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153120}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153400 :tutorial/content "When a function is presented as a graph, there is a useful graphical method to determine whether a function is an injection or not. This method is called the **horizontal line test.**\nHere is how it works:\n\n**Step 1.** Plot the graph of the function.\n\n**Step 2.** Draw a horizontal line on the plot.\n\n**Step 3.** Imagine moving the horizontal line up and down across the entire range.\n\n- If the horizontal line hits the graph only once no matter where we draw it, then the function is an injection. $\\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- If the horizontal line *ever* hits the function more than once, then the function is *not* an injection. $\\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's use the horizontal line test to determine whether the following function is an injection.\n\n![](images/77/77a23681c6f0f2bf4623017bafe70ca99f80eef9d7535188e2427bfd10a93035.png)\n\nIf we draw any horizontal line on the plot of the function above, it will hit the graph only once, no matter where we place the line.\n\n![](images/a9/a9afda7b5db1062bdb78d81c998def5deee0c118015e1c0486197f6460597ef0.png)\n\nTherefore, this function is an injection." :tutorial/id #uuid "eef22729-9c3b-5d13-bc5f-e473bb1cb189" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4314 :tutorial/title "The Horizontal Line Test"} :step/id #uuid "fccf2e16-8be5-596c-a284-ffed9d43209b" :step/math-academy-id 26215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153128}}] :activity/title "Injections" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152351}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046679} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407925} :activity/id #uuid "3676bd2b-76e7-5096-8724-d2aba2e73baf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051041 :topic/id #uuid "7c0a3263-cc36-57b6-839b-6ff5594cae61" :topic/math-academy-id 2678} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407922 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c583e6-ea60-5d7f-b515-6a1c05522f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407890} {:answer/id #uuid "486699da-5c52-50b7-a5e8-06a33ea08707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407895} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe41323-bdcb-5854-b702-4569e847d444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407908} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ef0e88-3d5a-58fe-a094-21c1a2d940b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407909} {:answer/id #uuid "d388ffe7-607b-5621-9d65-a25de45df1c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407910}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407908} :answer-field/id #uuid "dff19999-9a3e-55e9-8a46-2ed82f902f83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "58fa3eda-7303-530c-b266-fd43b4b16a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80370" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7c2f22af3b39c28d885ac490aae190e1b2d4ad55828a552fec031069d6d912a2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(0)=f(1)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(0)=0$ and $f(1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $R$ that map to the same element in $R.$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "2530907e-f808-5c7f-87ec-10398577b7b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407923}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407923 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "036d9afb-4836-58e3-9109-9dd285d1e857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407889} {:answer/id #uuid "3c173147-60ba-5870-bce4-c2f5db134f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407893} {:answer/id #uuid "4a28a6ba-2618-5929-9239-4e604586dd15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407896} {:answer/id #uuid "8a08c9ad-68d3-5048-9aa1-f2e7ffb89cb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407900} {:answer/id #uuid "8affdd3e-63cb-5945-abff-fdb11ff377f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407902}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407900} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e246480-5cbb-5dda-adb1-b0ac60280c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4c2e3f55-ea9b-5b9e-badf-26b9e5d93c01" :question/math-academy-id "q-80384" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0a08488ba59344033205903ca973870d0b10837295ced970bf307342e6f7241.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-3,3]\\to [-3,0]$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-3)=f(3)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [-3,3],$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the graph, we have $f(-3)=f(3)=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-3)=f(3)$ but $-3\\ne 3.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-3,3],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "59126e0e-e63f-5afb-b7f9-fc09c6d03a0e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27143f05-01a0-516c-bb47-d27121720b6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407891} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4e93b6-1460-51b3-854b-d70d0185543b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407894} {:answer/id #uuid "5f61eda3-2ef3-5e67-a98c-6effd83ff8d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407897} {:answer/id #uuid "76f0dd4e-ff74-50db-9f41-52a58331f2e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407899} {:answer/id #uuid "9b94f507-0ee4-574c-849e-434a6fd9142d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407905} :answer-field/id #uuid "6472838c-210f-5ad0-99a2-67a0bbd5ad6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1a3dbd42-cd16-5083-8842-33f0028feb3d" :question/math-academy-id "q-105207" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:[-2,4]\\to [-3,9]$ which is given by $f(x)=2x+1?$\n\n- $f(-2)=f(4)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in [-2,4],$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is an injection" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/5f/5fd931a87d6a5fb32d10ed931f443a8175f0760eba9fe2fe69336f8f79e9fd68.png)\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $f(2)=2(-2)+1=-3$ and $f(4)=2\\cdot 4+1=9.$\n\n- Statement II is true. As we can see from the graph, it passes the horizontal line test, meaning that there are no two distinct elements within $[-2,4]$ that map to the same element in $[-3,9].$ So, for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $[-2,4],$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since statement II is true, $f$ is an injection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9e89881c-dcee-5df4-a41c-0a7ef882a21c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407926}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c08a79-bba6-544e-a9ea-ca58b06c0451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407888} {:answer/id #uuid "8adf8b3d-ac7a-52cf-8b4c-03f7c2608d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407901} {:answer/id #uuid "9d38aa51-050c-524f-9c39-3aa01c15091e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407906} {:answer/id #uuid "dae716d0-1847-54c9-9892-a356fbf81aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407911} {:answer/id #uuid "e4388827-d9f0-5814-8367-e64cdc9001a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407901} :answer-field/id #uuid "67bb5409-539d-5019-8102-b7a8fb7859d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0c274592-717b-5063-8a47-5f35487eca60" :question/math-academy-id "q-80091" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9df0c9dd8b76ac337b6110fc92019924ad2ee1c4cc0da8216c27709b641a8451.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ whose graph is shown above?\n\n- $f(-2)\\ne f(0)$\n- $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$\n- $f$ is **one-to-one**" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection (also known as *one-to-one*) if *every* element in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the graph, we have $f(-2)=f(0)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Again, we have $f(-2)=f(0)$ but $-2\\ne 0.$ So, it's *not true* that for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ contained within the domain $R,$ if $f({x}_{1})$ and $f({x}_{2})$ are equal, then we must have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, since statement II is false, $f$ is not an injection (also known as *one-to-one*).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "d93d6add-1d7b-59c0-9993-38adf9231b4c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407922}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407926 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cfe54f-4c1a-5601-9bd2-0f1892a607c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407892} {:answer/id #uuid "60027804-dbc8-5ba3-b7bd-f44642116bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407898} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1d872d-1050-528d-989c-6e743504ef10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407903} {:answer/id #uuid "965d76be-2ba3-58ed-b51e-9ca0ad934ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407904} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5fab01-32db-5160-9eaf-32f4b30665c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407907}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407898} :answer-field/id #uuid "50962eb5-13e0-5527-99de-391f22e7ee03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "23e55f80-c083-57f4-820d-7aa2d67256ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-105210" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\n- $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=2x+3$\n- $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=(x-2)^{2}$\n- $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3 & =2{x}_{2}+3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- II is *not*  an injection.  For example, $g(1)=1=g(3),$ but $1\\ne 3.$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(0)=1=h(2\\pi ),$ but $0\\ne 2\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "f30d20c1-b6ec-5a82-941e-409bf138007b"}] :activity/title "Injections" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407887}]}
